<content>
  <section><title>Reading Exponential Graphs</title>
<para>The graph below describes the amount of caffeine, \(c\), in a person’s body \(t\) hours after an initial measurement of 100 mg.</para>
<media><image alt="Graph of function." src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c6505a870a48b5a86ecc8bf01f3b25c1613990ba"/></media><para>What does the point \(c(10)=35\) mean?</para>
<para>After 10 hours, there is 35 mg of caffeine in a person’s body.</para>
<para>What is the value of \(t\) when \(c(t)=80\)?</para>
<para>When the caffeine concentration is 80 mg, time is 2 hours, so \(t=2\).</para>
<title>Try It: Reading Exponential Graphs</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Using the graph above, what is the value of \(c(5)\), and what does it mean in the context of the situation?</para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para>Here is how to interpret this exponential graph:</para>
  <para>When \(t=5\), the curve crosses at 60, so \(c(5)=60\).</para>
  <para>This means that after 5 hours, the person has 60 mg of caffeine in their body.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
